  "state": {
    "case_id": "101",
    "case_summary": "A 59-year-old Caucasian woman presented with a mass in the upper outer quadrant of her left breast. She discovered the lump during self-examination a few months prior to her visit to our outpatient clinic. The mass was mobile, with no signs of skin involvement, and axillary lymph nodes were not palpable. A recent mammogram revealed a 3.5 cm lobulated, well-defined mass, with characteristics suggestive of a fibroadenoma and no indications of malignancy. Ultrasound imaging showed a solid, hypoechoic, well-circumscribed mass measuring 3.5 cm in diameter. Blood tests indicated elevated Ca 15-3 levels. The same lesion, though less than half its current size, was initially detected on a mammogram performed in her hometown four years earlier. At that time, it was not clinically palpable and was considered a long-standing benign fibroadenoma, so it was not removed. Comprehensive staging investigations, including liver function tests, chest x-ray, CT scans of the chest and abdomen, and bone scanning, all returned negative results.\n\nThe patient underwent an excisional biopsy, which revealed an invasive carcinoma. On macroscopic examination the tumour measured 3.5 cm in diameter, had solid composition and polymorphic appearance, with tan and brown, pale and hemorrhagic areas. Microscopic examination showed an invasive adenocarcinoma of the breast, characterized by average-sized cells, with well-defined borders and polygonal, rather than rounded contours. The neoplastic cells formed a matrix of solid, lobular, acinar and rarely papillary areas, with a fine vascular network in between. Foci of linear, trabecular and tubular growth patterns were visible. A few ducts with an intraductal (in situ) carcinoma of solid type were also noticed. The cytoplasm was clear and eccentrically placed and hyperchromatic nuclei with a low mitotic number (2 mitoses per 10HPF) were detected. Cells with mildly eosinophilic cytoplasma, nuclear pleomorphism and higher mitotic number were also seen. There was absence of necrosis and no lymphovascular invasion was noticed. On histochemical examination, many of the above cells were positive for PAS staining erased by diastase pre-treatment, keratins AE1/AE3, EMA and Cytokeratin 7, but negative for c-erb-b2 (score 0), Cytokeratin 20, melanosomes and vimentin. Markers of myoepithelial cells were also negative: smooth muscle actin-SMA and S-100 (only a few isolated positive cells). Staining for Chromogranin was positive in some cells and for Synaptophysin in most cells, indicating a degree of neuroendocrine activity of the tumor. The tumor was strongly positive for estrogen receptors (ER) and negative for progesterone receptors (PgR).\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
